# SQL and Migrations

## Migrations (goose, `db/migrations`)

- Append-only. Never edit a migration that has been committed; add a new one.
- Every migration has both `-- +goose Up` and `-- +goose Down`, and the Down must actually reverse the Up.
- Regenerate sqlc after any schema change (`make sqlc`, then `make sqlc-check`).

## Queries (sqlc, `db/queries`)

- Every query that filters, joins, or sorts needs an index justification: add a comment naming the index it relies on, or say why a scan is acceptable.
- Paginate lists with keyset pagination on `(published_at, id)`. Never `OFFSET`.
- Select explicit columns, not `SELECT *`.
- Use sqlc parameters (`$1`, `sqlc.arg(name)`); never build SQL by string concatenation.
- Tags are identified by `slug` (unique, lowercase, generated by the importer in Go). Look tags up and resolve conflicts by `slug`; `name` is display text only.

```sql
-- BAD: OFFSET gets slower the deeper the page
SELECT id, slug, title FROM posts ORDER BY published_at DESC LIMIT 20 OFFSET 1000;

-- BAD: `$1 IS NULL OR ...` can't be an index condition in a generic (prepared) plan.
-- The row comparison becomes a Filter and deep pages scan like OFFSET.
WHERE sqlc.narg(after_published_at)::timestamptz IS NULL
   OR (published_at, id) < (sqlc.narg(after_published_at)::timestamptz, sqlc.narg(after_id)::uuid)

-- GOOD: keyset, first page when the cursor args are NULL.
-- Index: posts_pagination_idx (published_at DESC, id DESC) WHERE published_at IS NOT NULL AND deleted_at IS NULL
-- name: ListPosts :many
SELECT id, slug, title, published_at
FROM posts
WHERE published_at <= now()
  AND deleted_at IS NULL
  -- A NULL cursor becomes a key that sorts before every row, so the row
  -- comparison is always present and always an index condition.
  AND (published_at, id) < (
    coalesce(sqlc.narg(after_published_at)::timestamptz, 'infinity'),
    coalesce(sqlc.narg(after_id)::uuid, 'ffffffff-ffff-ffff-ffff-ffffffffffff')
  )
ORDER BY published_at DESC, id DESC
LIMIT sqlc.arg(page_size);
```

Post state comes from timestamps; there is no `status` column. Draft: `published_at IS NULL`. Scheduled: `published_at > now()`. Published: `published_at <= now() AND deleted_at IS NULL`. Deleted: `deleted_at IS NOT NULL`.

The index is partial, so a query uses it only if its `WHERE` implies the index predicate. Always include `deleted_at IS NULL`; `published_at <= now()` already implies `published_at IS NOT NULL`. Keep `now()` in the query, never in an index predicate (index predicates must be immutable). Use `sqlc.narg` for the optional cursor so callers pass NULL for page 1; the sentinel lives in the SQL, not in Go. Never wrap an indexable condition in `param IS NULL OR ...`: check with a generic plan (`SET plan_cache_mode = force_generic_plan` plus `PREPARE`/`EXECUTE`) that it stays an `Index Cond`.

## Search

- Use `websearch_to_tsquery`, rank with `ts_rank_cd`.
- Apply `ts_headline` only to the top N ranked rows, never the full result set (it re-parses documents).
